{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=auckland}}
'''North Shore''' is a parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] that returns one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for North Shore is [[Simon Watts]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Simon Watts |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/watts-simon/ |website=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=19 October 2020}}</ref> who at the 2020 election was elected to succeed the retiring [[Maggie Barry]], also of National.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2019-11-05|title=National MP Maggie Barry announces retirement at 2020 election|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/117171219/national-mp-maggie-barry-announces-retirement-at-2020-election|access-date=2020-07-20|website=Stuff|language=en}}</ref>

==Population centres==
The [[1941 New Zealand census]] had been postponed due to World War II, so the 1946 electoral redistribution had to take ten years of population growth and movements into account. The [[North Island]] gained a further two electorates from the [[South Island]] due to faster population growth. The abolition of the [[country quota]] through the ''Electoral Amendment Act, 1945'' reduced the number and increased the size of rural electorates. None of the existing electorates remained unchanged, 27 electorates were abolished, eight former electorates were re-established, and 19 electorates were created for the first time, including North Shore.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=91–96}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/North Shore.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The boundaries of the North Shore electorate were last adjusted for the first election held using the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) voting system in {{NZ election link year|1996}}, when the number of general electorates decreased from 95 (1993) to 60 (1996), and the electorate expanded to the north into an area previously belonging to {{NZ electorate link|East Coast Bays}}. No boundary adjustments were undertaken in the subsequent redistributions in 2002, 2007,<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=14 September 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190123193826/https://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |archive-date=23 January 2019 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and 2013/14.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url=http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=4 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006075102/http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |archive-date=6 October 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The 2025 Boundary Review saw the electorate gain [[Windsor Park, New Zealand|Windsor Park]] from [[East Coast Bays (New Zealand electorate)|East Coast Bays]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

North Shore stretches up the eastern coast of [[North Shore City]] in [[Auckland]], starting in the south at [[Devonport, New Zealand|Devonport]] and moving northwards to take in [[Stanley Point]], [[Narrow Neck, New Zealand|Narrow Neck]], [[Bayswater, New Zealand|Bayswater]], [[Belmont, Auckland|Belmont]], [[Hauraki, Auckland|Hauraki]], [[Takapuna]], [[Lake Pupuke]], [[Milford, New Zealand|Milford]], [[Castor Bay]], [[Campbells Bay]], [[Sunnynook, New Zealand|Sunnynook]] and [[Mairangi Bay]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/north-shore-electorate-profile/ |title=North Shore - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 23 September 2025}}</ref> North Shore is predominantly [[European New Zealanders|European]] and [[Asian New Zealanders|Asian]], and has an average income high above the national average, boasting some of the most expensive real estate in the country.

==History==

The seat has been contested at every election in New Zealand since [[1946 New Zealand general election|1946]], and except for a single victory by future Labour Attorney-General [[Martyn Finlay]] in its first contest, has been safely held by the National Party ever since.

[[Wayne Mapp]] held the electorate from {{NZ election link year|1996}} until his retirement in 2011.<ref name="retire">{{cite web |work=[[Stuff.co.nz]] |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/4460226/National-MP-Wayne-Mapp-to-retire |date=15 December 2010 |access-date=4 October 2014 |title=National MP Wayne Mapp to retire |first=Liz |last=Willis}}</ref> He was succeeded by [[Maggie Barry]], who won the {{NZ election link year|2011}} and {{NZ election link|2014}}s.<ref name="North Shore 2011 election results" /><ref name='2014election'/>

